Delabella (the sweep-hull Delaunay triangulation BRepMesh's Delabella
algorithms take a plane's base triangulation from): the signs it decides
on computed exactly, and a hull that still comes out inconsistent ends the
triangulation as failed instead of a crash or an endless loop
(mitcad#846).

The case: a plane face, a square with a concave arc of a large radius cut
from a corner (1650 x 1650 mm, r = 1200 mm, the arc in 150 to 300 points).
Delabella lifts the points onto the paraboloid z = x^2 + y^2 and builds
their convex hull a point at a time: the faces the next point sees are
deleted, and new faces join it to their silhouette. Whether a face sees a
point is the sign of an in-circle determinant, which Delabella rounds
(long double: 64 bits of mantissa with GCC and Clang on x86, 53 with MSVC
and on ARM). The arc's points are nearly cocircular, and the rounded signs
contradict each other: the faces a point sees are no longer a disk (a
vertex on the silhouette twice, a face it does not see inside it). The
silhouette then has fewer edges than the new faces need, the face taken as
the last one added was never written in this step, and the silhouette walk
follows `next` and `sew` links left from earlier steps or never set: a
null or stray pointer (a crash), or a loop that never comes back to its
start (an endless loop at the walk's `while (pr != entry)`). With 53 bits
(Delabella's long double as double) the case's points crashed in three of
the face's four copies and a synthetic stand-in in two of four; with 64
bits, BRepMesh on the face with the radius swept from 400 to 1550 mm at
0.01 mm deviation crashed on 12 of 230 radii and looped on 2.

- incircleSign, orientSign: the signs of the in-circle and orientation
  determinants of the input coordinates, exact. The floating-point value
  is taken when it is larger than Shewchuk's error bound for it (doubled,
  for the fused multiply-adds a compiler may form); else the determinant
  is computed exactly as an expansion, a sum of doubles that do not
  overlap (J. R. Shewchuk, "Adaptive Precision Floating-Point Arithmetic
  and Fast Robust Geometric Predicates", 1997), with std::fma for the
  products' errors. Face::dot returns the in-circle sign (the triple
  product of the lifted points, without rounding the lifted z), and
  Face::cross the orientation's sign as the normal's z, which decides the
  Delaunay faces (z < 0). The normal's x and y are as before; they decide
  only for points in a line, in the start's contour of coplanar points.
  With exact signs every point is a vertex of the lifted hull, and the
  faces it sees are a disk.
- Guards for a hull that still comes out inconsistent (Inconsistent: an
  "[ERR]" message and 0, as when out of memory): a silhouette vertex met
  twice in one step (Vert::step), a number of new faces other than the
  deleted faces' plus 2, a silhouette walk that does not return to its
  start through every new face's first vertex (checked before anything is
  sewn), a neighbour not linked back to a deleted face, and no face seeing
  a point. The walk around the last point is bounded by the number of
  faces, and the fall-back search runs over the faces on the hull
  (Face::alive) instead of an index range. The face taken as the last one
  added is the last one added. Vertices and faces are allocated zeroed.
- The start on cocircular points (all points on one circle): the point
  retracted to become the cone's top is unlinked from the contour; the
  contour kept it, and the cone took more faces than there are.
- BRepMesh_DelabellaBaseMeshAlgo::buildBaseTriangulation throws
  Standard_Failure when Delabella returns 0, so that the face fails
  (MitCad's checked planes then triangulate it by the default insertion)
  instead of going on without a base triangulation.

Upstream: new.
